from lesson_writer import author,CPP

def w(name,purpose,steps,adapt,detail,exercise,limits):
 def tests(l):return detail+' The '+l(CPP/'support/ObjectOracle.java','Java object oracle')+', '+l(CPP/'support/objects.cpp','native harness')+' and '+l(CPP/'tools/object_differential.py','object driver')+' compare explicit observations and Java-generated wire round trips, trailing bytes and truncations. The inspected existing full-object result has 9,244 matches; that is not a per-file assertion count. Generic ERROR comparisons leave exact exception parity open.'
 author('src/org/minima/objects/'+name+'.md',purpose,steps,adapt,tests,exercise,limits+' The source map remains partial/not_verified.')
w('TxHeader','The durable metadata of a TxPoW: chain identity, time, block number, proof-of-work fields, ancestor links, MMR commitment and body hash.',[
 'Initialize the nonce/block number to zero, chain ID to MAIN_NET, time to the current clock, block difficulty to the maximal hash, default Magic and empty superparent hashes for every configured cascade level.',
 'Compress adjacent equal superparent IDs with run-length encoding. Write a one-byte count and one hash per run; the configured cascade-level count determines the total number to reconstruct, so no separate run count is written.',
 'The binary sequence is nonce, chain ID, time, block number, block difficulty, compressed superparents, MMR root/total, Magic, custom hash and body hash. Chain ID and difficulty use ordinary MiniData encoding; the commitment hashes use the hash-specific size limit.',
 'The reader expands each run into the existing superparent array until all levels are filled. A zero run consumes count/hash bytes without advancing the filled count. An oversized run reaches array bounds after partially writing Java entries.',
 'JSON presents the same runs with their final difficulty index and count. It also renders date from time.getAsLong using the environment’s local date/time formatting.',
 'The convenience MiniData converter catches IOException, logs and returns null. A header does not recalculate or authenticate its stored body hash; TxPoW and checking code supply that behavior.'
], 'Native code uses a vector sized at construction from GlobalParams and matching run grouping. It checks run overflow before expansion, which changes partial-mutation timing compared with Java. Date rendering uses localtime_r/strftime and corrects negative fractional seconds by flooring milliseconds to seconds. Native conversion catches all std::exception, wider than Java IOException.',
 'The header fixture crosses timestamps 0, -1, 99999999, 1788883200000 and 2147483648000 with superparent run parameters 0, 1, 2, 7 and 32. It fixes nonce/block number/body hash and compares JSON plus wire bytes. Both processes run with TZ=UTC, so passing date strings are evidence for that timezone and selected timestamps, not every locale or calendar date.',
 'Encode the conceptual ancestor list A,A,B,B,B as count/hash pairs. Why must changing GlobalParams.MINIMA_CASCADE_LEVELS after constructing an object be reviewed separately?',
 'Public Java reference fields and native value fields have different alias semantics. Malformed run counts, mutable globals, default-array references, date locale and converter logging still require full API review.')
w('Magic','The current and desired capacity parameters carried by the chain. Changes are intentionally gradual rather than jumping directly to a miner’s requested values.',[
 'Initialize size, KISS operations and transactions per block to 65536, 1024 and 256. Initialize the work threshold to floor((2^256 - 1)/10000), encoded as MiniData.',
 'For each capacity value, clamp the desired value to between half and twice the current value. Then apply its hard minimum. Order matters: the hard floor is applied after the relative clamp.',
 'Calculate the next current value as (16383*current + clampedDesired)/16384 using MiniNumber arithmetic. This gives the desired value a weight of only 1/16384.',
 'For the work threshold, perform analogous clamping and averaging with positive BigInteger arithmetic. Its hard bound is an upper bound on the numeric hash threshold, since a larger permitted hash threshold corresponds to easier work.',
 'Create the result as a fresh default Magic, then assign only its current fields. Desired fields in the returned object therefore reset to defaults; they are not copied from the receiver.',
 'checkSame compares only the four current fields. The three numeric capacities compare numerically; the MiniData work threshold compares exact bytes. Desired fields do not participate.',
 'Serialize all current fields first and all desired fields second, preserving decimal scale and work bytes. Reading restores those values without applying calculateNewCurrent or validating them.'
], 'The native counterpart reuses MiniNumber for decimal capacities and BigInt for the wide work threshold. A small shared weighted helper reproduces the three identical capacity branches. It retains the fresh-default result semantics and byte-sensitive work equality.',
 'The magic fixture starts from defaults, sets several desired triples including below-limit, doubled, very large and fractional requests, and crosses empty/zero/one/maximal work bytes. It compares both the original object and calculateNewCurrent output. This directly observes clamping, weighted results and returned defaults, but does not establish every nondefault-current state or checkSame branch.',
 'If current KISS operations is 1024 and desired is 2048, what is the next current value? Which desired value will the returned Magic hold unless a caller explicitly changes it again?',
 'The main method is a printed demonstration, not an assertion suite. Consensus callers must decide when current values are checked and desired values refreshed; this container alone does not establish valid chain parameter updates.')
